Package: polaris
Architecture: any
Depends:
 dde-control-center,
 dde-shell (>= 2.0.0),
 libdtk6declarative,
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${shlibs:Depends},
Recommends: polaris-sensord,
Description: System monitor for the dde-shell dock
 Polaris puts live system readings on the taskbar: CPU usage, CPU
 temperature, memory and swap usage, and the network transfer rate. Clicking
 the item opens a list of the busiest processes with a button to end them.
 .
 Values are read directly from /proc and /sys, so the dock item itself needs no
 daemon and no privileges. Which readings are shown and how often they refresh
 is set in the control centre, and stored with DConfig so the dock item and the
 control centre agree without any service of their own.

Package: polaris-sensord
Architecture: any
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${shlibs:Depends},
Description: Privileged sensor readings for Polaris
 The kernel publishes some readings to root only - the CPU package power counter
 in /sys/class/powercap is the one Polaris wanted first. Rather than opening that
 file to every user, this small service samples it and publishes the results as
 named values under /run/polaris/sensors, where the dock item picks them up.
 .
 It reads and publishes only, is sandboxed to sysfs and /run, and never accepts a
 path or a command from a client: the set of readings it can hand out is fixed at
 build time. The dock item works without it and falls back to whatever it can
 read itself.
